 more tightly related  to cell type and brain region than  individual genes and so this is just a  cartoon suggesting the strategy that as  we begin to get traction on single cell  analysis um that when and and we can use  chip seek and dnase hypersensitivity in  order to begin to identify regulatory  elements and that those regulatory  elements are likely to be distributed  based on the cells that we're looking at  and the time and development when we're  looking at them and so what we're hoping  then is that this is an orthogonal  approach when we find mutations that we  find likely damaging mutations either  near genes that we've already identified  as having autism risk or clustering  around genes that we have not yet  identified that that can begin to tell  us where the risk is specifically  relevant to autism or likely to be  specific to autism as opposed to being  more global and then you know once we  have an idea about if there's a cell  type a particular point in time at least  we have some traction less on the  temporal dimension but certainly to  certain extent on the cell type by then  modeling and demonstrating whether or  not in fact the mutation is having an  impact so again you could start to do  this simply by looking at all of our  high confidence genes and looking for de  novo mutations around them and seeing  whether or not those begin to tell you  when and where it looks like mutations  are more relevant for autism and this is  just a slide summarizing that but since  I'm down to less than five minutes it's  just basically what I said but what I  want to show you is and this is really  really preliminary data that should be  taken as really really preliminary data  more as sort of a conceptual example as  opposed to really anything that you  would want to you know hang your hat on  but this is just showing that when we  correct for father's age we see no  difference in de novo mutations in the  non-coding region in autism versus in  siblings this is a Simon simplex set  which has been so profitable looking at  at  fected vs. unaffected but and this is  only 40 families and we're now in the  midst actually working with mark mark  daily and David Goldstein and  dramatically expanding this sample to  about 1300 families from the Simon's  simplex collection but just as kind of  an initial look when we look at  enhancers in particular and and then  what we did is we decided well what's  what would be the equivalent of like a  loss-of-function mutation an enhancer  that you could interpret and we just  thought well if you lose more than one  base pair that's probably worse than  losing one base pair so we looked at in  dells and enhancers and we can begin to  see even in 40 people that we see in  over-representation and in dells but we  only see it in in frontal cortex so the  idea right behind this analysis is that  we can look at mutation burden when we  look across the entire brain we may get  no signal but we may be able to use the  distribution of non-coding variation to  begin to parse when and where in brain  that there's an over-representation  alright so I hope you get the sense that  if you didn't know before you walked in  the room that there's been a profound  change in the genetics of autism that we  have traction on on some very important  developmental processes as well as kind  of the centrality of F MRP as an autism  risk gene we have several points of  convergence both functionally but we  also can think about convergence in a  spatial temporal way really just to set  the stage for the next set of  experiments the the creation of  foundational data that's just all  happening now is going to help we  believe resolve some of these  developmental findings by giving us a  better underlying map of those  dimensions and then we hope that that  really does begin in the not-too-distant  future to begin to tell us about what  when and where this is relevant and sort  of what the level of analysis needs to  be in order to get traction I think what  our strategy now has evolved to is  really moving away from the idea that  genetic mutations are going to be  targets related a syrup utica  development really to the fact that  they're going to give us clues about  developmental trajectory that that's  going to lead us to an understanding of  the underlying circuitry and then that  maybe the level that we need to get to  in order to be able to intervene  for patients and families and I'm just  going to end with a very optimistic note  really it's hard for someone who's been  in this field for 20 years to not  believe that this is the very first  stages of a major revolution in  understanding and treating childhood  psychiatric disorders so thank you very  much for your attention
 

